INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A company has developed a new microservice using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. The Lambda function interacts with an Amazon DynamoDB table to retrieve user profiles. The development team observes occasional high latency during the initial invocations of the Lambda function, commonly known as ''cold starts''. To minimize cold start latency and improve the overall responsiveness of the API, which architectural change should the developer implement with the LEAST additional cost and operational overhead?', '["Initialize the AWS SDK client for DynamoDB outside the Lambda handler function in the global scope.","Configure Provisioned Concurrency for the AWS Lambda function to keep a specified number of execution environments warm.","Increase the memory allocated to the AWS Lambda function to reduce cold start times.","Implement a custom ''warm-up'' Lambda function that periodically invokes the main Lambda function to prevent cold starts."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most cost-effective and low-overhead solution for reducing cold start latency related to SDK client initialization. Initializing the AWS SDK client in the global scope (outside the handler function) allows the client to be reused across subsequent warm invocations of the same execution environment, reducing the overhead of establishing new connections and client setup on every invocation. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.1-001]. Option B (incorrect) Provisioned Concurrency effectively eliminates cold starts but incurs additional cost as you pay for the provisioned concurrency even when the function is idle. This violates the ''LEAST additional cost'' constraint. Option C (incorrect) increasing Lambda memory can sometimes reduce cold start times by allocating more CPU, but it also increases the cost per invocation. It''s a valid optimization but not necessarily the ''LEAST additional cost'' for this specific problem, especially if the primary issue is SDK initialization. Option D (incorrect) implementing a custom warm-up function adds operational overhead and complexity, requiring management of an additional Lambda function and its invocation schedule. This is generally less efficient than leveraging global scope initialization for SDK clients.', 28, 'm_28_1', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A developer has built a public-facing REST API using Amazon API Gateway that integrates with an AWS Lambda function. During peak traffic, users report intermittent `429 Too Many Requests` errors, indicating that the API is being throttled. The development team needs to ensure the API can handle bursts of traffic more gracefully and prevent service degradation for legitimate users, while maintaining control over the maximum invocation rate of the backend Lambda function. Which API Gateway feature should the developer configure?', '["Configure usage plans with API keys and associated throttle limits for individual clients.","Enable API Gateway stage-level throttling with a steady-state rate limit and a burst limit.","Implement AWS WAF rules to block IP addresses that exceed a certain request rate.","Increase the default concurrency limit for the AWS Lambda function to handle more simultaneous invocations."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most direct and effective way to manage overall API traffic and prevent throttling at the API Gateway level. Stage-level throttling allows you to set a default steady-state rate (requests per second) and a burst limit (maximum concurrent requests) for all requests to a specific API Gateway stage. This ensures that API Gateway buffers requests and prevents the backend Lambda function from being overwhelmed, handling bursts gracefully. Option A (incorrect) usage plans with API keys are primarily for monetizing APIs or controlling access for specific customers, not for general API-wide traffic management to prevent service degradation for all users during peak load. Option C (incorrect) implementing AWS WAF rules to block IP addresses is a security measure to protect against DDoS attacks or malicious bots, not a primary mechanism for managing legitimate, high-volume traffic and preventing throttling due to capacity limits. Option D (incorrect) increasing the default concurrency limit for the AWS Lambda function might help the Lambda function handle more requests, but it doesn''t prevent API Gateway from throttling if its own limits are hit first. More importantly, it doesn''t provide the same level of granular control over the API''s overall request rate as API Gateway''s built-in throttling.', 28, 'm_28_1', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A developer is creating several AWS Lambda functions for a new serverless application. Each function performs a specific task, such as processing image uploads, sending notifications, or performing database lookups. The team wants to follow best practices for cost optimization and efficient resource utilization. What is the MOST effective strategy for configuring the timeout settings for these Lambda functions?', '["Set the timeout for all Lambda functions to the maximum allowed value (15 minutes) to ensure no function ever times out.","Configure the timeout for each Lambda function to be slightly longer than its expected maximum execution time, plus a small buffer.","Use the default Lambda timeout of 3 seconds for all functions and rely on retries for any timeouts.","Implement custom logic within each Lambda function to monitor its own execution time and terminate if it exceeds a predefined threshold."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most effective strategy. Setting the timeout for each AWS Lambda function to be slightly longer than its expected maximum execution time, plus a small buffer, ensures that functions have enough time to complete their tasks without running unnecessarily long. This optimizes cost by preventing hung functions from consuming compute time and concurrency budget, directly addressing anti-pattern [ap-1.1-003]. Option A (incorrect) setting the timeout to the maximum 15 minutes for all functions is an anti-pattern [ap-1.1-003]. It can lead to increased costs if functions hang or enter infinite loops, as they will continue to consume resources for the full 15 minutes. It also ties up concurrency, potentially impacting other invocations. Option C (incorrect) using the default 3-second timeout for all functions is too aggressive for many real-world workloads and would lead to frequent, unnecessary timeouts and retries, degrading application performance and user experience. Option D (incorrect) implementing custom logic within the Lambda function to monitor execution time adds unnecessary complexity and operational overhead to the application code. AWS Lambda''s native timeout mechanism is designed to handle this efficiently.', 28, 'm_28_1', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A startup is building a new backend service that needs to expose a simple REST API for mobile clients. The service will primarily perform CRUD operations on an Amazon DynamoDB table. The team is small and prioritizes minimizing operational overhead and infrastructure management. Which combination of AWS services provides the LEAST operational overhead for exposing this API?', '["Amazon API Gateway (HTTP API) integrated with AWS Lambda functions.","Amazon API Gateway (REST API) with AWS Lambda functions and custom authorizers.","An Amazon EC2 instance running NGINX as a reverse proxy in front of a custom API application.","AWS App Runner to host a containerized API application with automatic scaling."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides the LEAST operational overhead. Amazon API Gateway HTTP APIs are designed for low-latency, cost-effective, and minimal-configuration API endpoints, making them ideal for simple REST APIs. Integrating directly with AWS Lambda functions creates a fully serverless backend where AWS manages all infrastructure, scaling, and maintenance. Option B (incorrect) using Amazon API Gateway REST API is a valid option, but HTTP APIs generally offer lower latency and cost for simpler use cases, and custom authorizers add complexity and operational overhead that might not be necessary for a ''simple REST API'' with ''LEAST operational overhead'' as the primary goal. Option C (incorrect) running NGINX on an Amazon EC2 instance requires significant operational overhead, including server provisioning, patching, scaling, and security management, which directly contradicts the requirement for ''LEAST operational overhead''. Option D (incorrect) AWS App Runner is a good option for containerized applications with minimal operational overhead, but for a simple REST API backed by Lambda, API Gateway HTTP API + Lambda is often even simpler to set up and manage, especially if the Lambda functions are already developed. App Runner is more suited when you have existing container images or require more control over the runtime environment.', 28, 'm_28_1', 4, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A company is migrating a legacy monolithic application to a microservices architecture using containers. The application consists of several independent services that need to be deployed, scaled, and managed efficiently. The development team wants to leverage a fully managed container orchestration service that minimizes infrastructure management tasks and provides automatic scaling capabilities, while also being cost-effective for varying workloads. Which AWS service is MOST suitable for this requirement?', '["Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate launch type.","Amazon ECS with Amazon EC2 launch type.","Amazon EKS with managed node groups.","Deploying containers directly on Amazon EC2 instances with Docker Compose."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate launch type is the MOST suitable option. AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that eliminates the need to provision, configure, and scale clusters of virtual machines. This significantly minimizes infrastructure management tasks and provides automatic scaling, aligning perfectly with the ''fully managed'' and ''minimizes infrastructure management'' requirements. It''s also cost-effective as you only pay for the resources consumed by your containers. Option B (incorrect) Amazon ECS with Amazon EC2 launch type requires you to manage the underlying EC2 instances, including patching, scaling, and security updates, which increases operational overhead compared to Fargate. Option C (incorrect) Amazon EKS with managed node groups is a fully managed Kubernetes service, which provides powerful orchestration capabilities. However, for a team prioritizing ''minimizing infrastructure management'' and ''cost-effective for varying workloads'' without the specific need for Kubernetes features, ECS with Fargate is generally simpler to operate and often more cost-effective. EKS still has a control plane cost and can be more complex to manage than ECS for simpler containerized workloads. Option D (incorrect) deploying containers directly on Amazon EC2 instances with Docker Compose requires manual management of EC2 instances, container orchestration, scaling, and high availability, which is the opposite of ''fully managed'' and ''minimizes infrastructure management tasks''.', 28, 'm_28_1', 5, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A developer is building an AWS Lambda function that needs to connect to an Amazon RDS database instance located within a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC). The Lambda function also requires several configuration parameters, such as database credentials and API keys for external services, to be securely passed at runtime without hardcoding them in the deployment package. Which combination of features should the developer use to meet these requirements securely and efficiently?', '["Configure the Lambda function to run within the VPC and use environment variables for database credentials and API keys.","Store database credentials and API keys in an Amazon S3 bucket and retrieve them at runtime, while connecting Lambda to the VPC.","Place the Lambda function in a public subnet and use AWS Secrets Manager to retrieve credentials, bypassing VPC configuration.","Hardcode sensitive information directly into the Lambda function''s code and configure a NAT Gateway for VPC access."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most secure and efficient approach. Configuring the AWS Lambda function to run within the VPC allows it to access private resources like an Amazon RDS database instance. Using environment variables is a standard and efficient way to pass configuration parameters to Lambda functions. For sensitive information like database credentials and API keys, environment variables can be encrypted at rest by AWS Lambda using AWS KMS, providing a secure method without hardcoding. Option B (incorrect) storing credentials in an Amazon S3 bucket and retrieving them at runtime is possible but adds complexity and latency compared to environment variables. While S3 can be secured, environment variables are designed for this purpose in Lambda. Option C (incorrect) placing a Lambda function in a public subnet is a security risk as it exposes the function to the public internet, which is not suitable for accessing private database resources. Bypassing VPC configuration would prevent access to the RDS instance. Option D (incorrect) hardcoding sensitive information directly into the Lambda function''s code is a severe security anti-pattern and should never be done. A NAT Gateway is used to allow private subnets to access the internet, not for Lambda to access VPC resources.', 28, 'm_28_1', 6, NULL, NULL);

