INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A developer is building an asynchronous processing system where user-uploaded images are placed into an Amazon S3 bucket, triggering an AWS Lambda function to process them. The Lambda function performs image resizing and metadata extraction. If the Lambda function fails to process an image due to transient errors or malformed input, the team needs to ensure that these failed messages are not lost and can be inspected later for debugging, without blocking the processing of other valid images. Which solution BEST addresses this requirement?', '["Configure the Lambda function''s execution role to allow S3 access and rely on default Lambda retries for failures.","Implement a try-catch block in the Lambda function code to log errors to Amazon CloudWatch Logs and then discard the message.","Set up an Amazon SQS queue as an intermediary between S3 and Lambda, configuring a dead-letter queue (DLQ) on the SQS queue.","Configure the AWS Lambda function with an asynchronous invocation destination for failures, routing them to an Amazon SQS dead-letter queue."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) is the most direct and efficient solution for handling asynchronous Lambda invocation failures. When an AWS Lambda function is invoked asynchronously (which is the case when triggered by Amazon S3 events), Lambda provides built-in support for destinations. You can configure a destination for failed invocations, such as an Amazon SQS queue, which acts as a dead-letter queue (DLQ). Lambda will automatically send details about failed invocations to this SQS queue after exhausting its internal retry attempts, ensuring messages are not lost and can be inspected. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.2-001] for SQS triggers, but applies the same principle for async invocation failures. Option A (incorrect) relying on default Lambda retries for S3-triggered asynchronous invocations will retry the function twice, but if it still fails, the event is discarded, leading to data loss. It doesn''t provide a mechanism to inspect failed messages. Option B (incorrect) logging errors to CloudWatch Logs is good for monitoring, but discarding the message means the failed event is lost and cannot be reprocessed or inspected without manual effort. Option C (incorrect) setting up an Amazon SQS queue as an intermediary between S3 and Lambda adds unnecessary complexity and latency. S3 can directly trigger Lambda functions, and Lambda''s asynchronous invocation destinations provide the required DLQ functionality more simply.', 28, 'm_28_2', 7, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A data science team is developing an AWS Lambda function that performs real-time inference using a large machine learning model. The model''s dependencies, including several large libraries, significantly increase the deployment package size, leading to slow cold start times and approaching the 250 MB unzipped deployment package limit. The team needs to optimize the Lambda function for faster cold starts and manage dependencies efficiently. Which approach should the developer take?', '["Package the large libraries into an AWS Lambda Layer and attach it to the Lambda function.","Increase the memory allocation for the Lambda function to improve cold start performance.","Store the large libraries in an Amazon S3 bucket and download them at runtime within the Lambda function handler.","Split the large machine learning model into smaller, separate Lambda functions, each with a subset of dependencies."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most effective approach for managing large dependencies and optimizing cold starts. Packaging large libraries into an AWS Lambda Layer allows these dependencies to be shared across multiple functions and reduces the size of the individual Lambda deployment package. Layers are cached by Lambda, which can significantly improve cold start times by not having to download the entire package every time. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.2-002]. Option B (incorrect) increasing memory allocation can sometimes reduce cold start times by providing more CPU, but it primarily impacts execution speed and cost. It does not directly solve the problem of large deployment package size or the associated download time during cold starts. Option C (incorrect) storing libraries in Amazon S3 and downloading them at runtime within the handler would significantly increase cold start latency, as the function would need to download the dependencies on every cold start, making performance worse. Option D (incorrect) splitting the model into smaller Lambda functions would increase complexity and operational overhead, and each function would still need its own set of dependencies, potentially leading to redundant packaging and management challenges.', 28, 'm_28_2', 8, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A developer has configured an AWS Lambda function to process events asynchronously, such as from Amazon SNS. The team needs to be notified immediately if any of these asynchronous invocations fail after all retry attempts are exhausted. They also want to capture the details of the failed event for further analysis. Which Lambda feature should the developer configure to achieve this with minimal custom code?', '["Implement a try-catch block in the Lambda function to send an Amazon SNS notification upon failure.","Configure an asynchronous invocation destination for failures on the Lambda function, routing to an Amazon SNS topic.","Set up an Amazon EventBridge rule to detect Lambda invocation errors and trigger an Amazon SNS topic.","Use Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to monitor Lambda error metrics and send notifications to an Amazon SNS topic."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most efficient and direct way to handle asynchronous Lambda invocation failures with minimal custom code. AWS Lambda''s asynchronous invocation destinations allow you to configure a target service, such as an Amazon SNS topic, for both successful and failed invocations. When a function fails after all retries, Lambda automatically sends the invocation record (including the event payload and error details) to the specified SNS topic, triggering immediate notifications and providing details for analysis. Option A (incorrect) implementing a try-catch block to send SNS notifications adds custom code and complexity to every Lambda function. It also doesn''t leverage the built-in retry mechanisms of asynchronous invocation before considering an event a ''failure''. Option C (incorrect) while Amazon EventBridge can react to various AWS events, configuring it to specifically detect and route Lambda invocation failures after all retries would be more complex than using the native Lambda Destinations feature, which is designed precisely for this use case. Option D (incorrect) using Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to monitor Lambda error metrics can provide notifications when error rates exceed a threshold, but it does not provide the specific details of the failed event payload, which is crucial for analysis, nor does it trigger ''immediately'' for a single failure after retries, but rather on aggregate metrics.', 28, 'm_28_2', 9, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A developer is optimizing an existing AWS Lambda function that performs data transformation. The function''s execution time varies significantly based on the input data size, and the team wants to find the optimal memory configuration to achieve the BEST balance between performance and cost. Which tool or approach is MOST effective for identifying this optimal configuration?', '["Manually test different memory settings in the AWS Management Console and monitor CloudWatch metrics for each configuration.","Use AWS X-Ray to trace the Lambda function''s execution and identify bottlenecks, then adjust memory based on X-Ray insights.","Leverage the AWS Lambda Power Tuning open-source tool or a similar automated approach to test various memory configurations.","Set the Lambda function''s memory to the maximum allowed (10,240 MB) to ensure the fastest possible execution, regardless of cost."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) leveraging the AWS Lambda Power Tuning open-source tool (or similar automated approaches) is the MOST effective for identifying the optimal memory configuration for cost-performance balance. This tool automatically invokes the Lambda function with various memory settings and analyzes the execution time and cost data from CloudWatch Logs to recommend the most cost-effective memory setting for a given performance target. Option A (incorrect) manually testing different memory settings is time-consuming, prone to human error, and less precise than an automated tool. Option B (incorrect) AWS X-Ray is excellent for tracing requests and identifying performance bottlenecks within the function''s code or downstream services, but it doesn''t directly help in determining the optimal memory setting for cost-performance balance across different memory configurations. Option D (incorrect) setting the Lambda function''s memory to the maximum allowed will likely result in the fastest execution but will also be the MOST expensive, directly violating the ''BEST balance between performance and cost'' constraint. It''s an inefficient use of resources if the function doesn''t require that much memory.', 28, 'm_28_2', 10, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A developer needs to create a simple, direct HTTP endpoint for an AWS Lambda function that will be invoked by a webhook from a third-party service. The primary requirements are to minimize setup complexity, avoid managing an API Gateway, and provide a secure, unique URL for the function. Which feature should the developer use?', '["Configure an Amazon API Gateway REST API and integrate it with the Lambda function.","Use an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to route traffic directly to the Lambda function.","Create an AWS Lambda Function URL for the function.","Deploy the Lambda function behind an Amazon CloudFront distribution with a custom origin."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) creating an AWS Lambda Function URL is the most straightforward solution. Lambda Function URLs provide a dedicated HTTP(S) endpoint for a Lambda function, allowing direct invocation without needing to configure Amazon API Gateway or an Application Load Balancer. This significantly minimizes setup complexity and operational overhead, aligning with the requirement for a ''simple, direct HTTP endpoint'' and ''avoid managing an API Gateway''. Option A (incorrect) configuring an Amazon API Gateway REST API is a valid way to expose a Lambda function via HTTP, but the requirement specifically states to ''avoid managing an API Gateway'' due to its additional complexity compared to a direct Function URL. Option B (incorrect) using an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to route traffic to a Lambda function is possible, but it introduces the overhead of managing an ALB, which is more complex than a Function URL for a single function endpoint. Option D (incorrect) deploying the Lambda function behind an Amazon CloudFront distribution adds a CDN layer, primarily for caching and global distribution, which is overkill and adds complexity for a simple webhook endpoint. CloudFront would typically sit in front of an API Gateway or ALB, not directly a Lambda function URL for this use case.', 28, 'm_28_2', 11, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A developer is building a new AWS Lambda function that processes customer orders. The function needs to access configuration values such as a database connection string, an external API endpoint, and a feature flag. These values might change over time and should be easily updated without redeploying the Lambda function code. The team also wants to ensure these values are not hardcoded and can be managed securely. Which Lambda feature should be used?', '["Store the configuration values directly in the Lambda function''s deployment package.","Use AWS Lambda environment variables to store and retrieve the configuration values.","Fetch configuration values from an Amazon S3 bucket at every invocation of the Lambda function.","Embed the configuration values as constants within the Lambda function''s source code."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) using AWS Lambda environment variables is the most appropriate feature. Environment variables allow you to pass configuration settings to your Lambda function code without modifying the code itself. They can be easily updated via the Lambda console, CLI, or SDK without redeploying the function, meeting the ''easily updated without redeploying'' requirement. For sensitive data, AWS encrypts environment variables at rest using AWS KMS, providing a secure way to manage them. Option A (incorrect) storing configuration values directly in the deployment package means that any change to these values would require a full redeployment of the Lambda function, which is inefficient and violates the ''easily updated without redeploying'' requirement. Option C (incorrect) fetching configuration values from an Amazon S3 bucket at every invocation would introduce unnecessary latency and cost, especially for frequently accessed values. While S3 can store configuration, environment variables are designed for direct, low-latency access within Lambda. Option D (incorrect) embedding configuration values as constants within the source code is a form of hardcoding. This is an anti-pattern as it makes updates difficult, requires redeployment for every change, and is insecure for sensitive information.', 28, 'm_28_2', 12, NULL, NULL);

