INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A development team is building a serverless application using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. They need to automate the deployment process, including unit and integration testing, to ensure code quality and rapid releases. The solution must provide a repeatable and consistent way to deploy both the Lambda functions and their associated infrastructure. Which approach offers the LEAST operational overhead for this requirement?', '["Implement an AWS CodePipeline that uses AWS CodeBuild for testing and AWS SAM to define and deploy the Lambda functions and API Gateway endpoints.","Manually deploy Lambda functions via the AWS Management Console and configure API Gateway using the console, then run tests from a local machine.","Develop custom scripts to package Lambda code, upload it to Amazon S3, and then use AWS CloudFormation to deploy the S3 artifacts and API Gateway.","Set up an AWS CodePipeline that uses AWS CodeDeploy for Lambda deployments and manually manages API Gateway configurations through separate scripts."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides the most efficient and low-overhead solution. AWS SAM (Serverless Application Model) simplifies the definition of serverless applications, including Lambda functions and API Gateway, as infrastructure as code. AWS CodePipeline orchestrates the entire CI/CD workflow, with AWS CodeBuild executing automated tests and AWS SAM handling the deployment, ensuring repeatability and consistency with minimal manual intervention. Option B (incorrect) involves significant manual effort, which directly contradicts the goal of automation and increases operational overhead. Option C (incorrect) requires developing and maintaining custom scripts for packaging and deployment, adding to operational overhead compared to using AWS SAM''s built-in capabilities. Option D (incorrect) uses AWS CodeDeploy for Lambda, which is valid, but it separates API Gateway management into ''separate scripts,'' increasing complexity and potential for inconsistencies compared to a unified SAM template.', 30, 'm_30_3', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A company is updating a critical AWS Lambda function that processes customer orders. They need to deploy the new version with minimal risk of downtime or impact to users, allowing for a quick rollback if issues are detected. The deployment strategy must gradually shift traffic to the new version and automatically revert if errors occur. Which AWS service and strategy should be used to meet these requirements?', '["Use AWS CodeDeploy with an all-at-once deployment strategy, manually monitoring for errors and rolling back if necessary.","Configure AWS Lambda aliases with a canary deployment strategy, shifting traffic incrementally and using CloudWatch alarms for automatic rollbacks.","Deploy the new Lambda version directly, then update the API Gateway integration to point to the new version, relying on manual testing.","Utilize AWS Elastic Beanstalk to deploy the Lambda function, configuring its built-in rolling updates feature for gradual traffic shifting."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) directly addresses the requirements for minimal risk, gradual traffic shifting, and automatic rollback. AWS Lambda aliases, in conjunction with AWS CodeDeploy, support canary (linear or canary) deployments where traffic is shifted incrementally (e.g., 10% every 10 minutes) to the new Lambda version. CloudWatch alarms can monitor metrics like error rates, triggering an automatic rollback if thresholds are breached. Option A (incorrect) uses an all-at-once deployment, which is risky for critical production updates as it can cause downtime if the new version fails, violating anti-pattern [ap-3.3-001]. Option C (incorrect) involves manual steps and relies on manual testing, which is prone to human error and does not provide automated rollback capabilities. Option D (incorrect) is not suitable as AWS Elastic Beanstalk is primarily for deploying web applications on EC2 instances or Docker containers, not directly for managing AWS Lambda functions.', 30, 'm_30_3', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A team manages a microservices application running on Amazon ECS. They need to deploy updates to their services with zero downtime and the ability to quickly revert to the previous working version if any issues arise. The deployment process should involve spinning up new tasks with the updated code alongside the old tasks before shifting traffic. Which AWS CodeDeploy deployment type is MOST suitable for this scenario?', '["All-at-once deployment, where all instances are updated simultaneously.","Rolling update deployment, where a batch of instances is updated at a time.","Blue/Green deployment, where a new environment is provisioned and traffic is shifted.","In-place deployment, where the application on existing instances is stopped and updated."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) describes a Blue/Green deployment strategy, which is ideal for zero-downtime deployments on Amazon ECS. With Blue/Green, a completely new environment (''Green'') running the updated application version is provisioned alongside the existing production environment (''Blue''). Traffic is then gradually or instantly shifted from ''Blue'' to ''Green'' via a load balancer. If issues are detected, traffic can be instantly rolled back to the ''Blue'' environment, minimizing impact. Option A (incorrect) is an all-at-once deployment, which causes downtime and high risk if the new version fails, violating anti-pattern [ap-3.3-001]. Option B (incorrect) is a rolling update, which updates instances in batches, but it still has a period where both old and new versions are running, and rollback can be more complex than a Blue/Green swap. Option D (incorrect) is an in-place deployment, which would cause downtime as the application is stopped and updated on existing instances.', 30, 'm_30_3', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_28_all_1775006086746', 'A software company needs to ensure that their serverless application environments (development, staging, production) are identical in terms of infrastructure configuration. They want to automate the provisioning and updating of these environments to minimize configuration drift and ensure consistency. Which AWS service should they use to define and manage their infrastructure as code, integrated with their CI/CD pipeline?', '["AWS CloudFormation, using templates to define all resources and deploying them via AWS CodePipeline.","AWS Management Console, manually configuring resources in each environment to match a documented standard.","AWS Systems Manager, using Run Command to execute scripts for resource creation and updates across environments.","AWS OpsWorks, leveraging Chef cookbooks or Puppet manifests to manage server configurations and application deployments."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the best approach for defining and managing infrastructure as code (IaC) to ensure consistent environments. AWS CloudFormation allows developers to define all AWS resources (Lambda, API Gateway, DynamoDB, etc.) in templates. These templates can then be version-controlled and deployed consistently across development, staging, and production environments, often orchestrated by AWS CodePipeline. This minimizes configuration drift and provides a repeatable deployment process. Option B (incorrect) is a manual approach that is error-prone, time-consuming, and directly leads to configuration drift, failing the consistency requirement. Option C (incorrect) is primarily for managing instances and executing commands, not for defining and provisioning entire infrastructure stacks as code. Option D (incorrect) is an older configuration management service primarily focused on EC2 instances and on-premises servers, and while it supports IaC concepts, CloudFormation is the more modern and comprehensive AWS-native solution for general infrastructure provisioning.', 30, 'm_30_3', 4, NULL, NULL);

